<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paolo Veronese</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1528–1588</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paolo Veronese was a painter from Verona who worked mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, known for his huge, brightly coloured feasts and allegories. His works include The Wedding at Cana and the ceilings of the Doge's Palace. In 1573 he was questioned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> over a Last Supper filled with secular figures and retitled it The Feast in the House of Levi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paolo-veronese.jpg" alt="Paolo Veronese" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Veronese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paolo_Veronese,_avtoportret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
